--TEST--
`Core\Request::body` answers a `tainted string`, so a request's own bytes reach a grammar sink only through the launderer that names it — spec § 15, `rule:security/tainted-qualifier` and `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`, `rule:http-server/a-part-is-consumed-in-one-of-three-ways`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The qualifier is the third thing § 15's signature says, and the only one no
// runtime assertion can show: a plain `string` is assignable into a `tainted`
// target, so a case that only declared one would pass over a member that had
// dropped the mark. What pins it is the refusal -- a body is the least trusted
// thing a request carries, so `rule:security/sink-predicate`'s grammar sinks are shut to it.
tainted string $raw = Core\Request::body();
Core\Regex\Pattern $pattern = Core\Regex::compile($raw);
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
  --> case.nvs:8:51
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
